[{:db/id 17592186124592 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a024553e-c27e-5c68-a828-a6ec8223e683"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124403 :question/problem "Consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $\\mathbb R$ with degrees at most $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\big\\{ 0,t^2 \\big\\}$ is a linearly independent set in $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$\n\nII. $-5t^2$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\big\\{ 0, t^2 \\big\\}$\n\nIII. $\\big\\{ 0, t^2 \\big\\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124489 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ac0d302b-9680-52bd-b447-e8dfc12a3304"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124302 :question/problem "Consider the vector space $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2 \\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ![](images/e9/e98eb00ac7ee6ffb83d4a902d92e9bda4434e6bfaeae1fb91a210849d3e1991f.svg) is a linearly independent set in $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ![](images/71/716877f60112610eae19b094ca691edf0c0d2ecdffc54dc0b785d66c96e64909.svg)\n\nIII. ![](images/cc/ccd021603e72ceb60be92eabec243bfdd5ce33eb22a6b801030a05efc02a861d.svg) is a basis of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n![](images/a1/a16ee652347a67e66994875711dff835418bfcfb4f85a058a80f9164af525cab.svg)\nis true if and only if $x_1=x_2=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n![](images/f1/f173928232bbfd7680c2952250c78e7ba354bd4851cb4783bd531cc683044db5.svg)\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n![](images/74/7449707a6aa218f65cc22db3a56e8ed9391e3b5e562301e77fd62cb2f3dad20a.svg)\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry $a_{11} = 0\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/b9/b952b253393b4d14572b41ad1ca265e3b858edeec36e4c937f644621ae497294.svg)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124490 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2dd6c5c5-53ed-575f-8c9f-dc822f51b9e6" "question-c626f6f5-d2a8-58e2-a687-8abd5e7a0913"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124303 :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ be a basis of a vector space $V.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2},- \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}$ is a basis of $V$\n\nII. $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0}  \\right\\}$ spans $V$\n\nIII. $\\left\\{  -\\mathbf{b_2}, 3\\mathbf{b_1}\\right\\}$ is linearly dependent" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathcal{B}$ is a basis, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors in $V,$ and\n\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ spans $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{2}+{x}_{2}(-{b}_{1})+{x}_{3}({b}_{3}+{b}_{1}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{2}+({x}_{3}-{x}_{2}){b}_{1}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n![](images/62/625e812c25818cec68c6a5bcd59a39945feb09526607244e42059ed8af6f9b08.svg)\nAlso, $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2},- \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $\\mathcal{B}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2},- \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx_1\\mathbf{b}_1+x_2\\mathbf{b}_2+x_3\\mathbf{b}_3\n=\nx_2\\mathbf{b}_2+(x_3-x_1)(-\\mathbf{b}_1)+x_3 (\\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1})\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. In order for $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0}  \\right\\}$ to span $V,$ every element of $\\mathcal{B}$ must be able to be expressed as a linear combination of elements of $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0}  \\right\\}.$ But the vector $\\mathbf{b}_1 \\in \\mathcal{B}$ can't be written as a linear combination of the elements of $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0}  \\right\\}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. If $\\left\\{  -\\mathbf{b_2}, 3\\mathbf{b_1}\\right\\}$ is linearly dependent then there exist $x_1$ and $x_2$ not both zero such that\n\n$$\nx_1(-\\mathbf{b_2}) + x_2(3\\mathbf{b_1}) = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nThen, we would have\n\n$$\n(3x_2) \\cdot \\mathbf{b_1} + (-x_1) \\cdot \\mathbf{b_2} + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{b}_3= \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nBut this would mean that $\\mathcal{B} = \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ is linearly dependent, which contradicts the fact that $\\mathcal{B}$ is a basis of $V.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186360516 :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $3.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{0,{t}^{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{3}[t]$\n\nII. $-{t}^{3}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{0,{t}^{3}\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{0,{t}^{3}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}_{3}[t]$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186360514 :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $3.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{0,t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{3}[t]$\n\nII. $3{t}^{2}-t$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{0,t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{0,t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}_{3}[t]$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186360513 :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{t,{t}^{2}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{2}[t]$\n\nII. ${t}^{2}+1$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{t,{t}^{2}\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{t,{t}^{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}_{2}[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot t+{x}_{2}\\cdot {t}^{2}=0,\n$$\n\n \nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that all linear combinations of\n\n$$\n\\{t,{t}^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nresult in polynomials without the constant term.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, the polynomial ${t}^{2}+1$ can't be written as a linear combination of the set  $\\{t,{t}^{2}\\}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-a024553e-c27e-5c68-a828-a6ec8223e683" :question/id #uuid "a024553e-c27e-5c68-a828-a6ec8223e683" :question/math-academy-id "q-107130" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{2,{t}^{2}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{2}[t]$\n\nII. $2+{t}^{2}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{2,{t}^{2}\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{2,{t}^{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}_{2}[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot 2+{x}_{2}\\cdot {t}^{2}=0,\n$$\n\n \nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n2+{t}^{2}=1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot {t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the polynomial $t$ can't be written as a linear combination of\n\n$$\n\\{2,{t}^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nsince all these combinations result in polynomials without terms of the form $at.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-55e1e424-505b-5097-b514-ba9bc2cc90d9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-55e1e424-505b-5097-b514-ba9bc2cc90d9" :answer-field/id #uuid "55e1e424-505b-5097-b514-ba9bc2cc90d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e75c2b7e-4724-50f4-baef-5d83a5a25436" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e75c2b7e-4724-50f4-baef-5d83a5a25436" "answer-18a10dfc-afa4-52d6-9bf7-b580f906a755" "answer-299a0ce7-32c9-5434-88c4-7d63337cd0bd" "answer-6e05ed0b-0f3c-5c51-9fcd-1cb42a9bc2e9" "answer-84245521-5afe-5940-8979-375abcac5604"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e75c2b7e-4724-50f4-baef-5d83a5a25436" :answer/id #uuid "e75c2b7e-4724-50f4-baef-5d83a5a25436" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18a10dfc-afa4-52d6-9bf7-b580f906a755" :answer/id #uuid "18a10dfc-afa4-52d6-9bf7-b580f906a755" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-299a0ce7-32c9-5434-88c4-7d63337cd0bd" :answer/id #uuid "299a0ce7-32c9-5434-88c4-7d63337cd0bd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e05ed0b-0f3c-5c51-9fcd-1cb42a9bc2e9" :answer/id #uuid "6e05ed0b-0f3c-5c51-9fcd-1cb42a9bc2e9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84245521-5afe-5940-8979-375abcac5604" :answer/id #uuid "84245521-5afe-5940-8979-375abcac5604" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186360515 :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $3.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{1,t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{3}[t]$\n\nII. ${t}^{3}-3t+8$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{1,t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{1,t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}_{3}[t]$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125245 :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186406882 :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186360523 :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186406881 :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-4)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{21}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & c \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-ac0d302b-9680-52bd-b447-e8dfc12a3304" :question/id #uuid "ac0d302b-9680-52bd-b447-e8dfc12a3304" :question/math-academy-id "q-107374" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-matrix is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]=0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1cfb4b04-4369-55b5-b86c-1bcf8c68a19d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1cfb4b04-4369-55b5-b86c-1bcf8c68a19d" :answer-field/id #uuid "1cfb4b04-4369-55b5-b86c-1bcf8c68a19d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8a3f2158-ddfb-537c-810a-54533dd0a0c4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bf2dcfe2-dcba-5af7-8a16-53d83c9d1420" "answer-8867cc1f-ad13-5b27-82c7-d9621d2bc854" "answer-6e6cbcc7-075a-5d4e-95fb-61a97ecac1f1" "answer-8a3f2158-ddfb-537c-810a-54533dd0a0c4" "answer-cd6d4c15-e447-5d3b-bdaf-eabf62f4dcbd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf2dcfe2-dcba-5af7-8a16-53d83c9d1420" :answer/id #uuid "bf2dcfe2-dcba-5af7-8a16-53d83c9d1420" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8867cc1f-ad13-5b27-82c7-d9621d2bc854" :answer/id #uuid "8867cc1f-ad13-5b27-82c7-d9621d2bc854" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e6cbcc7-075a-5d4e-95fb-61a97ecac1f1" :answer/id #uuid "6e6cbcc7-075a-5d4e-95fb-61a97ecac1f1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a3f2158-ddfb-537c-810a-54533dd0a0c4" :answer/id #uuid "8a3f2158-ddfb-537c-810a-54533dd0a0c4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd6d4c15-e447-5d3b-bdaf-eabf62f4dcbd" :answer/id #uuid "cd6d4c15-e447-5d3b-bdaf-eabf62f4dcbd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186244095 :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ and $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ be bases of ${R}^{3}$ and ${R}^{2}$, respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n\nII. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n\nIII. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$, any vector of ${R}^{2},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This means that $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x\\}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\n- Statement II is false. The set $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the vector ${b}_{3}$ is in ${R}^{3}$ but it is not a linear combination of  ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ as the set  $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. This means that the set does not span ${R}^{3}$ and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\n- Statement III is false. The set $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1}\\}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-2dd6c5c5-53ed-575f-8c9f-dc822f51b9e6" :question/id #uuid "2dd6c5c5-53ed-575f-8c9f-dc822f51b9e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-107537" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n\nII. $\\{{b}_{1},2{b}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n\nIII. $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{2}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}(2{b}_{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+(2{x}_{2}){b}_{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, $\\{{b}_{1},2{b}_{2}\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{{b}_{1},2{b}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}={x}_{1}{b}_{1}+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2}{b}_{2}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. The set $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the third vector is a linear combination of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This means that the set is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f2baa79b-54b3-5737-acdd-b3964add504b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f2baa79b-54b3-5737-acdd-b3964add504b" :answer-field/id #uuid "f2baa79b-54b3-5737-acdd-b3964add504b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-013e97ca-b56e-5aee-9c9f-d304a8a1dbd7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1b5613e5-d85e-5aa4-87d6-6ad009986011" "answer-c794a74a-633c-5598-9aae-0206903fc293" "answer-4eb0c223-1e6e-53a8-9673-4bcd81bc316b" "answer-013e97ca-b56e-5aee-9c9f-d304a8a1dbd7" "answer-384b6eb0-e2bf-5f86-af7f-3398204e70e4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b5613e5-d85e-5aa4-87d6-6ad009986011" :answer/id #uuid "1b5613e5-d85e-5aa4-87d6-6ad009986011" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c794a74a-633c-5598-9aae-0206903fc293" :answer/id #uuid "c794a74a-633c-5598-9aae-0206903fc293" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4eb0c223-1e6e-53a8-9673-4bcd81bc316b" :answer/id #uuid "4eb0c223-1e6e-53a8-9673-4bcd81bc316b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-013e97ca-b56e-5aee-9c9f-d304a8a1dbd7" :answer/id #uuid "013e97ca-b56e-5aee-9c9f-d304a8a1dbd7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-384b6eb0-e2bf-5f86-af7f-3398204e70e4" :answer/id #uuid "384b6eb0-e2bf-5f86-af7f-3398204e70e4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124862 :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},x\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n\nII. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n\nIII. $\\{{c}_{3},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$, any vector of ${R}^{2},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}.$ This means that $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},x\\}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\n- Statement II is false. The set $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\n- Statement III is true. The set $\\{{c}_{3},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because, by reordering the vectors, we obtain the basis $C.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-c626f6f5-d2a8-58e2-a687-8abd5e7a0913" :question/id #uuid "c626f6f5-d2a8-58e2-a687-8abd5e7a0913" :question/math-academy-id "q-107941" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of a vector space $V.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent\n\nII. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},2{b}_{3}\\}$ is a basis for $V$\n\nIII. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{3},{b}_{1}+{b}_{3}\\}$ is linearly dependent" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ is a basis, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors in $V,$ and\n\n- $B$ spans $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since if $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent then there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}({b}_{1}+{b}_{2})=0,\n$$\n\nwhere at least one of $x$'s is not zero. But then \n\n$$\n({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}){b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+0{b}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that $B$ is linearly dependent. This would contradict the fact that $B$ is a basis.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}(2{b}_{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+(2{x}_{3}){b}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},2{b}_{3}\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},2{b}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}={x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+\\frac{{x}_{3}}{2}(2{b}_{3})\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the third vector is a linear combination of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4e2c51c7-77b4-56a2-ab4a-cf386667088e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4e2c51c7-77b4-56a2-ab4a-cf386667088e" :answer-field/id #uuid "4e2c51c7-77b4-56a2-ab4a-cf386667088e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3bffe33d-8459-5da2-bb30-4e33f3a9a3a3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6188c2a2-db52-5316-ac0b-78dce45a1839" "answer-3777d47d-2620-5df7-9137-5fe91751144e" "answer-68ec88dd-c105-5380-bc0a-502cd62f7812" "answer-3bffe33d-8459-5da2-bb30-4e33f3a9a3a3" "answer-bebe80cf-1236-5ad0-9caa-015b17550bea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6188c2a2-db52-5316-ac0b-78dce45a1839" :answer/id #uuid "6188c2a2-db52-5316-ac0b-78dce45a1839" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3777d47d-2620-5df7-9137-5fe91751144e" :answer/id #uuid "3777d47d-2620-5df7-9137-5fe91751144e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68ec88dd-c105-5380-bc0a-502cd62f7812" :answer/id #uuid "68ec88dd-c105-5380-bc0a-502cd62f7812" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3bffe33d-8459-5da2-bb30-4e33f3a9a3a3" :answer/id #uuid "3bffe33d-8459-5da2-bb30-4e33f3a9a3a3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bebe80cf-1236-5ad0-9caa-015b17550bea" :answer/id #uuid "bebe80cf-1236-5ad0-9caa-015b17550bea" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186244093 :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n\nII. $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n\nIII. $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{3}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1}\\}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the set contains a vector from another vector space.\n\n- Statement II is false. The set $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{2}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}({c}_{1}+{c}_{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}){c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}{c}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{3}\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $C$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}{c}_{3}=({x}_{1}-{x}_{3}){c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}({c}_{1}+{c}_{3})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125765 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$ denote the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $\\mathbb{R}$ that have degree at most $n.$ Also, let $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ be the vector space of all $2 \\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}.$ Then, we have the following important standard bases.\n\n- The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_1[t]$ is $\\{ 1, \\: t \\}.$\n\n- The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ is $\\{ 1, \\: t, \\: t^2 \\}.$\n\n- The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$ is $\\{ 1, \\: t, \\: \\ldots, \\: t^n \\}.$\n\n- The standard basis of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is\n![](images/11/1178fd622498d7466d7bddc4232ecc3cc9a9d9a8a9362766918069ba43110055.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124709 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125350 :step/math-academy-id 653375 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125527 :step/math-academy-id 653376 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125351 :step/math-academy-id 653379 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125352 :step/math-academy-id 653382 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125355 :step/math-academy-id 653383 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125353 :step/math-academy-id 653386 :db/ensure [:step/validate]}]
